Instruction: Read the following excerpt of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the "ability to consummate" provision in this merger agreement subject to the carveouts identified in the Material Adverse Effect definition?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: "Material Adverse Effect" is defined as any change, event, or circumstance that (i) has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the overall operations, assets, or financial condition of the Company and its consolidated subsidiaries, or (ii) would, on aggregate, prevent or materially delay the Company's ability to consummate the merger. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following effects shall not be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Effect with respect to clause (ii):
(a) any detrimental effect arising from general fluctuations in the global economy or broad movements in market conditions that do not disproportionately impact the Company relative to other industry participants;
(b) any changes resulting from modifications in U.S. or international accounting standards or interpretations thereof;
(c) any force majeure events, including acts of God, natural disasters, or governmental responses to pandemics, unless such events result in consequences that are significantly more adverse than those experienced by peers;
(d) any effects from regulatory changes or legislative modifications that affect the industry as a whole;
(e) any adverse impact solely attributable to the announcement or public disclosure of this Agreement.
Furthermore, the clause pertaining to preventing or delaying consummation shall operate independently of the carveouts listed in paragraphs (a) through (e), such that only events directly impairing operational capabilities and transaction-specific conditions will be evaluated under the consummation provision. (Pages 12-14)
